## Abstract

The goal of this project was to create a multiplayer party game around the theme of pirates, titled Party Plunder. This game needed to consist of three smaller games. Our team designers came up with a list of many choices for mini-games.

The first chosen is Fat King Chase: stuck on a desert island, the four players must gain as much gold from the King as possible to win. The King is running around the island, and only holding him will increase your gold count, but if you drop him due to another player or a trap, your gold count will start to decrease until you have the King again.

The second game is Captain’s Call: Cleaning: set on a pirate ship, the players must clean up the piles of dirt that have accumulated around the ship. Cleaning a spot will give the player a point. Unlike Fat King Chase, your points won’t decrease, and the player with the most points wins.

The final game is Oh Ship: this game is based on one found in a carnival. The players are lined up and, using cannons, must shoot at the targets, which are little ships, to gain points. But be careful—hitting the sea serpents will make them steal some of your points. Uniquely, this game has no movement besides that of the cannon’s aiming.

The approach to create the three games was to split up tasks between the four of us in the group—two designers and two developers—to keep things moving as a team, using Discord and in-person meetings to give constant updates on when each of us had completed any work so we all knew what had and had yet to be completed.

The two designers, Tommy and Sasha, split the tasks of creating levels, objects, characters, and animations in half so that Tommy focused on all to do with the first and third game and Sasha on the second and 2D art, but both helped each other throughout.

Myself and Lillian split the games as a whole to make things less confusing and to avoid conflicts when merging work. I worked on the first and second game while Lillian worked on the third and the main menu/lobby. This did not go to plan, as neither the main menu, lobby, nor third game had been created. Therefore, Sasha took care of the main menu, and I dealt with the multiplayer within the games and the third game. We left the lobby for if we had time, but the creation of the third game was done quickly within the final two weeks of the ten because it was not initially my task to complete, and I had only been made aware of Lillian’s lack of work just before the final two weeks. This limited time frame to complete the final game is why it is not as polished as the others could be.

